WEBVTT

NOTE R7 phrase-balanced reflow. Timing within measured narration sections remains approximate; no forced word alignment.

1
00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:07.147 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
A coding agent sends several long requests.

2
00:00:07.147 --> 00:00:09.439 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
A person asks one short question.

3
00:00:09.439 --> 00:00:11.731 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Both share the same model servers.

4
00:00:11.731 --> 00:00:14.939 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
The servers are working,
but the person is waiting.

5
00:00:14.939 --> 00:00:17.537 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
The problem is not necessarily the model.

6
00:00:17.537 --> 00:00:19.802 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
It can be how the service admits work,

7
00:00:19.802 --> 00:00:22.579 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
selects a server, and keeps requests moving.

8
00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.736 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Follow three decisions.

9
00:00:25.736 --> 00:00:28.171 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Can this request proceed now?

10
00:00:28.171 --> 00:00:30.606 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Which server should receive it?

11
00:00:30.606 --> 00:00:34.539 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
During an upgrade, will its
processing path remain compatible?

12
00:00:34.539 --> 00:00:38.473 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Flow control, routing, and rollout
coordination answer different questions.

13
00:00:38.473 --> 00:00:41.657 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
A new destination does not create capacity.

14
00:00:41.657 --> 00:00:45.590 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
A good queue cannot fix an
incompatible processing path.

15
00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:49.972 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
llm-d already supported cache-aware routing

16
00:00:49.972 --> 00:00:52.445 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
and separate prefill and decode pools.

17
00:00:52.445 --> 00:00:55.126 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Version 1.0, announced on October twentieth,

18
00:00:55.126 --> 00:00:57.806 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
puts more emphasis on
operating that service:

19
00:00:57.806 --> 00:01:01.724 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
shared traffic, stable configuration,
and coordinated upgrades.

20
00:01:01.724 --> 00:01:06.256 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
It works with engines
such as vLLM and SGLang.

21
00:01:06.256 --> 00:01:09.924 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
It is not a new language model.

22
00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.343 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
With a configured policy,

23
00:01:13.343 --> 00:01:17.337 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
requests can enter separate
flows by tenant and priority.

24
00:01:17.337 --> 00:01:21.927 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Dispatch selects priority first,
then shares turns within a band.

25
00:01:21.927 --> 00:01:28.513 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
At saturation, work can wait at the router
instead of piling into busy server queues.

26
00:01:28.513 --> 00:01:31.506 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
The default still resembles
first-come, first-served.

27
00:01:31.506 --> 00:01:33.701 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
These policies require configuration.

28
00:01:33.701 --> 00:01:36.695 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
They do not create extra capacity.

29
00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:40.716 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Now follow one agent request.

30
00:01:40.716 --> 00:01:43.216 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Server A holds its cached prefix.

31
00:01:43.216 --> 00:01:46.048 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Returning there can avoid
repeated prompt work.

32
00:01:46.048 --> 00:01:49.547 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
But A may be busy while B has room.

33
00:01:49.547 --> 00:01:52.380 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
The router considers
cache locality and load.

34
00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:56.546 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
There is a trade-off: waiting for
reuse versus sending work elsewhere.

35
00:01:56.546 --> 00:01:59.378 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Cache-aware does not mean always choose A.

36
00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.749 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Prefill processes the prompt.

37
00:02:02.749 --> 00:02:04.647 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Decode produces output tokens.

38
00:02:04.647 --> 00:02:07.582 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Separate pools let these
phases scale independently.

39
00:02:07.582 --> 00:02:10.171 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Follow the same request through both.

40
00:02:10.171 --> 00:02:15.867 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Its state must reach the decode side;
it does not start an unrelated job there.

41
00:02:15.867 --> 00:02:20.527 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
This separation can help resource use,
but it also adds coordination work.

42
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:25.241 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Suppose a new serving
revision is rolling out.

43
00:02:25.241 --> 00:02:27.532 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
A ready prefill component is not enough

44
00:02:27.532 --> 00:02:30.448 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
if its matching decode
component is unavailable.

45
00:02:30.448 --> 00:02:32.339 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
With the rollout screener configured,

46
00:02:32.339 --> 00:02:35.318 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
revision-aware routing keeps
a request inside one revision.

47
00:02:35.318 --> 00:02:38.173 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
The current guide marks this plugin Alpha.

48
00:02:38.173 --> 00:02:42.372 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
The old pair keeps serving while
the new pair becomes ready.

49
00:02:42.372 --> 00:02:46.571 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Coordinated rollout is the lesson—not a
promise that every failure disappears.

50
00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.759 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Consider llm-d when you
operate shared model capacity

51
00:02:52.759 --> 00:02:56.553 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
and face bursty traffic, repeated
context, or split-pool upgrades.

52
00:02:56.553 --> 00:02:59.081 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
If one server already meets your needs,

53
00:02:59.081 --> 00:03:02.989 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
more infrastructure may add
work without solving a problem.

54
00:03:02.989 --> 00:03:05.012 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
First name the constraint.

55
00:03:05.012 --> 00:03:07.034 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Then choose the control.

56
00:03:07.034 --> 00:03:09.057 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
Faster inference is useful.

57
00:03:09.057 --> 00:03:12.183 line:92% position:50% align:center size:92%
A predictable service
needs an operating policy.
